-5ad8-862e-1c4f1b286f41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545813 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider $∠2$ and $∠3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/50/50d1e010a8279bf8f59a5e57b4ee4d4b744af7e431351bb17397e123aa79c59a.png)\n\nNote that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${100}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2={100}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠3$ and the angle whose measure is ${36}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠3={36}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ce/ce9944a3fa28177bd0882fccd53d544159b6f041c570e7037cac7db31103130e.png)\n\nThe angles $∠v$, $∠2,$ and $∠3$ combined give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠v+m∠2+m∠3 & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v+{100}^{∘}+{36}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v+{136}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v & ={44}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545828 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545828 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117567" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545828 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545828 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/47/4745f9e651eb50c345550e83ee82eb957cd28fd82e3398b667e4285bb512194c.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{AB}∥\\overset{⟷}{CD}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545828 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d28d4f3-c7e7-5aa9-96b5-112441d6c1ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545828 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the angles $∠JID$ and $∠EJB$ are corresponding angles, they are congruent and therefore have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠JID & =m∠EJB \\\\ 4x+{25}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have an equation that we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+{25}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘}-x \\\\ 5x+{25}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 5x & ={155}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{{155}^{∘}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} & ={31}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={31}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545835 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545835 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53171" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545835 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545835 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/db/db1b39e3f00fd6bb64ed738fb12213f4775d2bd7d098dfa9d0efec2192d7a64f.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545835 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90e45430-ff4c-59cd-b8f8-806fae001939" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545835 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider $∠2$ and $∠3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a7/a775b7c9ce63aa5b9f8c03fb91e7018d2f1edde5a2a2acdb099ae5ba53a874e4.png)\n\nNote that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${75}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2={75}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠3$ and the angle whose measure is ${50}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠3={50}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](images/01/01462502a4fbe0b25e489cc611200beb47cd926b8a055b69b7279aa0f301850b.png)\n\nThe angles $∠1$, $∠2,$ and $∠3$ combine to give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2+m∠3 & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{75}^{∘}+{50}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{125}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1 & ={55}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545939 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186546002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545939 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6804ab59-29d8-51c1-a53a-33027de8ef79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545939 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186545813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545956 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186545939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545956 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9cbce6de-8a3c-5419-8c14-0e187454a2b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545956 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186545835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546002 #edb/ref 1062 #uui